[{"id":84793,"sender_id":43,"sender_full_name":"DavidRoberts","avatar_url":"https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/fbc2dbfb28f9e9275a4fd4cd109b5a21?d=identicon&version=1","content":"<p><a href=\"#narrow/channel/15-Mathematics.2C-Physics-.26-Philosophy/topic/locally.20small.20category/near/84793\">#20</a> I have a technical question about the definition of large but locally small categories when working over ZF(C). One could define such a thing as a class <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{Obj}</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8889em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathrm\">Obj</span></span></span></span></span> of objects and a class <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{Mor}</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathrm\">Mor</span></span></span></span></span> of arrows, such that <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mspace></mspace><mspace width=\"0.1111em\"/><mo lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0.17em\"></mo><mtext> ⁣</mtext><mo lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">:</mo><mspace width=\"0.3333em\"/><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>→</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">j</mi></mrow><mo>×</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">j</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(s,t)\\colon {\\mathrm{Mor}} \\to {\\mathrm{Obj}} \\times {\\mathrm{Obj}}</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">s</span><span class=\"mpunct\">,</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.1667em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">t</span><span class=\"mclose\">)</span><span class=\"mspace nobreak\"></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.1111em;\"></span><span class=\"mpunct\"></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:-0.1667em;\"></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.1667em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mrel\">:</span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.3333em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathrm\">Mor</span></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">→</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8889em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathrm\">Obj</span></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">×</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8889em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathrm\">Obj</span></span></span></span></span></span> has sets for fibres. Or one could define it as set-enriched category with a proper class <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{Obj}</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8889em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathrm\">Obj</span></span></span></span></span>, where there is given a hom-set for each ordered pair of objects. I assume we can take these hom-sets disjoint (there was a discussion about this at some point, I can’t recall where), but the bigger problem is going from the second description to the first. Only if the hom-sets are defined in a uniform way do we get a proper class containing all the morphisms. Is there any risk, if you like, of a large category of interest falling through the gaps? That is, we have a large category such that we <strong>cannot</strong> gather all its morphisms into a single class?</p>","timestamp":1595931302,"subject":"locally small category","stream_id":15,"display_recipient":"Mathematics, Physics & Philosophy","reactions":[]},{"id":84794,"sender_id":563,"sender_full_name":"Hurkyl","avatar_url":"https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/ff42efa94f111c3f310350c872de031d?d=identicon&version=1","content":"<p><a href=\"#narrow/channel/15-Mathematics.2C-Physics-.26-Philosophy/topic/locally.20small.20category/near/84794\">#21</a> You can replace a category with an isomohpic one whose homsets are disjoint by setting <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover accent=\"true\"><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\">~</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">{</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>f</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>∣</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>hom</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\widetilde{\\mathrm{hom}}(x, y) = \\{ (x, f, y) \\mid f \\in \\hom(x, y) \\}</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1.2044em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord accent\"><span class=\"vlist-t\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.9544em;\"><span style=\"top:-3em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathrm\">hom</span></span></span><span class=\"svg-align\" style=\"top:-3.6944em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:3em;\"></span><span style=\"height:0.26em;\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"100%\" height=\"0.26em\" viewBox=\"0 0 600 260\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><path d=\"M200 55.538c-77 0-168 73.953-177 73.953-3 0-7\n-2.175-9-5.437L2 97c-1-2-2-4-2-6 0-4 2-7 5-9l20-12C116 12 171 0 207 0c86 0\n 114 68 191 68 78 0 168-68 177-68 4 0 7 2 9 5l12 19c1 2.175 2 4.35 2 6.525 0\n 4.35-2 7.613-5 9.788l-19 13.05c-92 63.077-116.937 75.308-183 76.128\n-68.267.847-113-73.952-191-73.952z\"/></svg></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">x</span><span class=\"mpunct\">,</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.1667em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0359em;\">y</span><span class=\"mclose\">)</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mopen\">{(</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">x</span><span class=\"mpunct\">,</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.1667em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.1076em;\">f</span><span class=\"mpunct\">,</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.1667em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0359em;\">y</span><span class=\"mclose\">)</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">∣</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8889em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.1076em;\">f</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">∈</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mop\">hom</span><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">x</span><span class=\"mpunct\">,</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.1667em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0359em;\">y</span><span class=\"mclose\">)}</span></span></span></span>.</p>\n<p>As for your question, I presume the devil is in the details of being “given a hom-set”. But if you can formulate the predicate “<span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>:</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>→</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f : x \\to y</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8889em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.1076em;\">f</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">:</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.4306em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">x</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">→</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.625em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0359em;\">y</span></span></span></span>”, then the proper class of morphisms is given by the predicate “<span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∃</mi><mi>x</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\exists x,y</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8889em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">∃</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">x</span><span class=\"mpunct\">,</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.1667em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0359em;\">y</span></span></span></span> such that <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>:</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>→</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f : x \\to y</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8889em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.1076em;\">f</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">:</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.4306em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">x</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">→</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.625em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0359em;\">y</span></span></span></span>”. Similarly, if you can formulate the predicate “<span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0576em;\">S</span></span></span></span> is the hom-set of morphisms from <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.4306em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">x</span></span></span></span> to <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.625em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0359em;\">y</span></span></span></span>”, then the predicate <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>:</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>→</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f : x \\to y</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8889em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.1076em;\">f</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">:</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.4306em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">x</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">→</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.625em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0359em;\">y</span></span></span></span> is given by “<span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∃</mi><mi>S</mi><mo>:</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\exists S : f \\in S</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">∃</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0576em;\">S</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">:</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8889em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.1076em;\">f</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">∈</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0576em;\">S</span></span></span></span> and <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0576em;\">S</span></span></span></span> is the hom-set of morphisms from <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.4306em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">x</span></span></span></span> to <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.625em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0359em;\">y</span></span></span></span>”.</p>","timestamp":1595935424,"subject":"locally small category","stream_id":15,"display_recipient":"Mathematics, Physics & Philosophy","reactions":[]},{"id":84797,"sender_id":10,"sender_full_name":"Mike Shulman","avatar_url":"https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/ede34282f405f902e12fa7e6cf336572?d=identicon&version=1","content":"<p><a href=\"#narrow/channel/15-Mathematics.2C-Physics-.26-Philosophy/topic/locally.20small.20category/near/84797\">#22</a> If we work in NBG, which is basically the same as working in ZF(C) but with more reification, then we would have an actual proper-class-function <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo>:</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">j</mi></mrow><mo>×</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">b</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">j</mi></mrow><mo>→</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{Mor} : \\mathrm{Obj}\\times {\\mathrm{Obj}} \\to {\\mathrm{Set}}</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathrm\">Mor</span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">:</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8889em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathrm\">Obj</span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">×</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8889em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathrm\">Obj</span></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">→</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathrm\">Set</span></span></span></span></span></span> and we should be able to do the same thing we do with small categories.</p>","timestamp":1595950895,"subject":"locally small category","stream_id":15,"display_recipient":"Mathematics, Physics & Philosophy","reactions":[]},{"id":84800,"sender_id":43,"sender_full_name":"DavidRoberts","avatar_url":"https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/fbc2dbfb28f9e9275a4fd4cd109b5a21?d=identicon&version=1","content":"<p><a href=\"#narrow/channel/15-Mathematics.2C-Physics-.26-Philosophy/topic/locally.20small.20category/near/84800\">#23</a> @Mike, sure, but I was checking ZF(C). I’m making sure my approach works in multiple systems.</p>\n<p>@Hurkyl, thanks, I get the general idea. I was wondering about what one might call the ‘usual’ large categories that arise in practice: algebras for a(n essentially) algebraic theory or a monad, or an operad, or sheaves or .... I don’t think there’s any risk, but wanted to check. Presumably if one has it for Set then the rest follow?</p>","timestamp":1595972270,"subject":"locally small category","stream_id":15,"display_recipient":"Mathematics, Physics & Philosophy","reactions":[]},{"id":84803,"sender_id":415,"sender_full_name":"Oscar_Cunningham","avatar_url":"https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/881c3dd9dbf7d3988e5cd546cd9501a2?d=identicon&version=1","content":"<p><a href=\"#narrow/channel/15-Mathematics.2C-Physics-.26-Philosophy/topic/locally.20small.20category/near/84803\">#24</a> I always assumed that when people talked about classes in ZFC they were implicitly using NBG. There’s no such thing as a class within ZFC! Am I missing something?</p>","timestamp":1596022902,"subject":"locally small category","stream_id":15,"display_recipient":"Mathematics, Physics & Philosophy","reactions":[]},{"id":84804,"sender_id":43,"sender_full_name":"DavidRoberts","avatar_url":"https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/fbc2dbfb28f9e9275a4fd4cd109b5a21?d=identicon&version=1","content":"<p><a href=\"#narrow/channel/15-Mathematics.2C-Physics-.26-Philosophy/topic/locally.20small.20category/near/84804\">#25</a> You can treat a class as a syntactic object, but considered up to extension, I guess. A formula is not an object of ZFC, but they can be manipulated with similar operations as for sets: Cartesian products, disjoint unions (both finite), functions, quotients by equivalence relations (using Scott’s trick if needed). Maybe it’s just people working with the model of NBG that arises from the sets and classes of ZFC, so only classes defined by formulas, not “arbitrary” classes.</p>","timestamp":1596026501,"subject":"locally small category","stream_id":15,"display_recipient":"Mathematics, Physics & Philosophy","reactions":[]},{"id":84805,"sender_id":10,"sender_full_name":"Mike Shulman","avatar_url":"https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/ede34282f405f902e12fa7e6cf336572?d=identicon&version=1","content":"<p><a href=\"#narrow/channel/15-Mathematics.2C-Physics-.26-Philosophy/topic/locally.20small.20category/near/84805\">#26</a></p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Maybe it’s just people working with the model of NBG that arises from the sets and classes of ZFC</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Yeah, my understanding is that those are basically equivalent.  So if NBG “works”, then so does ZFC by using this model.</p>","timestamp":1596040455,"subject":"locally small category","stream_id":15,"display_recipient":"Mathematics, Physics & Philosophy","reactions":[]},{"id":100733,"sender_id":17,"sender_full_name":"zskoda","avatar_url":"https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/0ea9c6065e45ce1c059bd086dadd928e?d=identicon&version=1","content":"<p><a href=\"#narrow/channel/15-Mathematics.2C-Physics-.26-Philosophy/topic/locally.20small.20category/near/100733\">#27</a> An object <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">A</span></span></span></span> in a category <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0715em;\">C</span></span></span></span> is called a <a href=\"https://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/locally%20small%20object\">locally small object</a> if the slice category <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C/A</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0715em;\">C</span><span class=\"mord\">/</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">A</span></span></span></span> is essentially small (has a small skeleton). In the setup of module categories and abelian categories \na stronger notion is (widely) called a locally small category, namely a category such that all its objects are locally small. That is, <a href=\"https://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/subobject\">subobject</a>s of any object <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">A</span></span></span></span> (isomorphism classes of monomorphisms with target <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">A</span></span></span></span>) form a set. E.g. 5.23.2 in</p>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/Carl%20Faith\">Carl Faith</a>, <em>Algebra: Rings, Modules, Categories</em>, vol. 1,  Grundlehren der mathematischen Wissenschaften <strong>190</strong>, Springer (1973) [<a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-642-80634-6\">doi:10.1007/978-3-642-80634-6</a>]</li>\n</ul>\n<p>and 1.2 in</p>\n<ul>\n<li>N. Popescu, <a href=\"https://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/Abelian%20categories%20with%20applications%20to%20rings%20and%20modules\">Abelian categories with applications to rings and modules</a>, London Mathematical Society Monographs 3, Academic Press 1973, xii + 470 pp</li>\n</ul>\n<p>I do not know how to nlabify this. It is important as it is quite standard in that subject.</p>\n<p>P.S. I understand that locally small categories are also called <a href=\"https://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/well-powered%20category\">well-powered</a> in nonabelian contexts.</p>","timestamp":1661709630,"subject":"locally small category","stream_id":15,"display_recipient":"Mathematics, Physics & Philosophy","reactions":[]},{"id":100735,"sender_id":17,"sender_full_name":"zskoda","avatar_url":"https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/0ea9c6065e45ce1c059bd086dadd928e?d=identicon&version=1","content":"<p><a href=\"#narrow/channel/15-Mathematics.2C-Physics-.26-Philosophy/topic/locally.20small.20category/near/100735\">#28</a> Jeremy Rickard wrote at <a href=\"https://math.stackexchange.com/questions/3657704/abelian-categories-with-generator-objects-are-locally-small\">stackexchange/abelian-categories-with-generator-objects-are-locally-small</a></p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Using “locally small” for what is now usually called “well-powered” wasn’t particularly idiosyncratic for the time. It was once quite common terminology and used in some well-known early books such as Mitchell’s “Theory of Categories”. In Mac Lane’s “Categories for the Working Mathematician” he explicitly states that he avoids using the term “locally small” at all because of the two conflicting meanings.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Working mathematicians in module categories and abelian categories are still often using this terminology, especially when saying <a href=\"https://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/locally%20small%20abelian%20category\">locally small abelian category</a>.</p>","timestamp":1661710535,"subject":"locally small category","stream_id":15,"display_recipient":"Mathematics, Physics & Philosophy","reactions":[]},{"id":100750,"sender_id":10,"sender_full_name":"Mike Shulman","avatar_url":"https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/ede34282f405f902e12fa7e6cf336572?d=identicon&version=1","content":"<p><a href=\"#narrow/channel/15-Mathematics.2C-Physics-.26-Philosophy/topic/locally.20small.20category/near/100750\">#29</a> “Well-powered” means that the posets of subobjects are small, which is a much weaker statement than that the whole slice category <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C/A</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0715em;\">C</span><span class=\"mord\">/</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">A</span></span></span></span> is essentially small.  Did you really mean the latter?</p>","timestamp":1661750921,"subject":"locally small category","stream_id":15,"display_recipient":"Mathematics, Physics & Philosophy","reactions":[]},{"id":100752,"sender_id":11,"sender_full_name":"Urs","avatar_url":"https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/b00d1ee17463af676c78974439b92471?d=identicon&version=1","content":"<p><a href=\"#narrow/channel/15-Mathematics.2C-Physics-.26-Philosophy/topic/locally.20small.20category/near/100752\">#30</a> That’s the same mistake that yesterday I had fixed in another entry: <a href=\"#narrow/channel/2/topic/locally.20small.20object/near/100736\">here</a>. (In the reference which Zoran cited there it is stated correctly.)</p>","timestamp":1661761768,"subject":"locally small category","stream_id":15,"display_recipient":"Mathematics, Physics & Philosophy","reactions":[]},{"id":100804,"sender_id":17,"sender_full_name":"zskoda","avatar_url":"https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/0ea9c6065e45ce1c059bd086dadd928e?d=identicon&version=1","content":"<p><a href=\"#narrow/channel/15-Mathematics.2C-Physics-.26-Philosophy/topic/locally.20small.20category/near/100804\">#31</a> Oh, that is my mistake, of course I meant monomorphisms (I was lost once in formalizing that the simplest way). Though I dislike calling it poset if it is a class to start with and specially if one does not take the equivalence classes but the category where there are possibly equivalent ones (hence a preorder rather than a partial order),</p>","timestamp":1661855464,"subject":"locally small category","stream_id":15,"display_recipient":"Mathematics, Physics & Philosophy","reactions":[]},{"id":100807,"sender_id":17,"sender_full_name":"zskoda","avatar_url":"https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/0ea9c6065e45ce1c059bd086dadd928e?d=identicon&version=1","content":"<p><a href=\"#narrow/channel/15-Mathematics.2C-Physics-.26-Philosophy/topic/locally.20small.20category/near/100807\">#32</a> There is an entire circle of entries related to localization in Abelian categories which I am trying to improve these days, and for next few days please consider them as <em>work in progress</em> till certain batch of work is finished. The main problem as usual is that this is an area where there is plethora of (equivalent or equivalent under some additional conditions) formalisms and competing or contradictory terminologies in the literature. One of the reasons people often rediscover known things. I am reviewing one paper in Mathematical Reviews on the same topic and also revising a preprint (a joint work) with slight variant on the topic. Not only that the entire paper under MR (published in Comm. Alg.) consists of results known for 50 years but it is not a single excursion of the authors, they have an earlier work in 2014 or so where they have another half of the work, so after 8 years nobody told them that it is known, and Zbl review does not note either (I wrote to the Zbl author, no response, people just write surface level reviews and do not pay attention to the published literature).</p>","timestamp":1661858047,"subject":"locally small category","stream_id":15,"display_recipient":"Mathematics, Physics & Philosophy","reactions":[]},{"id":100818,"sender_id":10,"sender_full_name":"Mike Shulman","avatar_url":"https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/ede34282f405f902e12fa7e6cf336572?d=identicon&version=1","content":"<p><a href=\"#narrow/channel/15-Mathematics.2C-Physics-.26-Philosophy/topic/locally.20small.20category/near/100818\">#33</a> I would say “the poset of subobjects is small” or “the preorder of monomorphisms is essentially small”.  That is, “a subobject” is an isomorphism class of monos with fixed codomain.</p>","timestamp":1661896454,"subject":"locally small category","stream_id":15,"display_recipient":"Mathematics, Physics & Philosophy","reactions":[]},{"id":100868,"sender_id":17,"sender_full_name":"zskoda","avatar_url":"https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/0ea9c6065e45ce1c059bd086dadd928e?d=identicon&version=1","content":"<p><a href=\"#narrow/channel/15-Mathematics.2C-Physics-.26-Philosophy/topic/locally.20small.20category/near/100868\">#34</a> Yes, I tend to think of subobject as an isomorphism class but it is hard, in my experience, to impose that discipline on the community.</p>","timestamp":1661968934,"subject":"locally small category","stream_id":15,"display_recipient":"Mathematics, Physics & Philosophy","reactions":[]}]